          "answer": "Guidance agrees that diet is a core part of managing high blood pressure. Every major body recommends the same set of changes, alongside any medication. The main ones are:\n\n- **Less sodium (salt).** This is the most-studied dietary change for blood pressure. [removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.] Most sodium comes from packaged and restaurant food, not the salt shaker, so reading labels helps more than any single swap. Any reduction helps. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **A DASH-style eating pattern.** This pattern emphasises vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts and vegetable oils, and limits saturated fat, fatty meat, sweets and sugary drinks. It works as a whole pattern, not by adding one \"blood pressure food.\" Combined with sodium reduction, the effect is larger than either alone. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*; *Hypertension: when treatment starts*.)\n- **More potassium, from food.** Higher potassium intake is associated with lower blood pressure. The described route is food, not supplements. Potassium-based salt substitutes are not suitable for everyone, especially people with kidney disease or on medicines that raise potassium, so ask a clinician first. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **Less alcohol.** Alcohol raises blood pressure in a dose-related way, and cutting back lowers it. Commonly stated limits are up to two drinks a day for men and one for women, with less being better. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- **Weight.** Weight reduction is recommended where body weight is above the healthy range. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts*.)\n\n**How strong the evidence is.** The sources I retrieved describe sodium reduction as the most-studied change and the DASH pattern as backed by trials. They describe the potassium and alcohol links with more cautious wording (\"associated,\" \"dose-related\"). They do not give formal evidence grades for the hypertension diet advice, so I can't rank these changes more precisely than that.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The sources point to a disagreement about *when* the diagnosis and treatment start, not about the diet itself:\n- United States guidance classes 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 mm Hg as stage 1 hypertension. European guidance begins hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and treats that band as \"high-normal.\" The same reading is labelled differently depending on which guidance is used. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds*.)\n- Because of this, bodies also differ on how hard to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. Much of the group that US guidance calls stage 1 is advised on lifestyle alone by European guidance. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts*.)\n\nThe sources don't give a single agreed sodium target from one named body, so I've reported the commonly stated figures rather than choosing between them.\n\nThe advice above is general education. Your clinician can help you work out which targets and changes fit your own situation, especially if you have kidney disease or take medicines.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
